{
 "id": "2025-ji-hunhe",
 "name": "Hunhe River Streets in Shenyang: Perceived Vitality (1–5)",
 "description": "Method replication: participants rate the perceived vitality of 20 street scenes along the Hunhe River in Shenyang on a 1–5 scale from low vitality to high vitality. Each scene shows one spot in four directions; the images are not the paper's stimuli (38 Baidu street views fetched for this template along both banks between Changqing Bridge and Shengli Bridge, 2020–2022 captures).\n\nOriginal study: Ji, Fu, Shi & Liu (2025), \"Measurement and influencing factors of urban waterfront street vitality from the perspectives of behavior and perception\", Environmental Research Communications 7, 015041. 40 evaluators with backgrounds in urban planning, architecture or landscape architecture rated Baidu street views of 297 points on 59 segments of the Hunhe waterfront streets with a semantic differential (low vitality – high vitality, 5 points), in a lab slide show of up to 10 s per image; segment means were compared with behavioural vitality from Baidu heat maps.\n\nDifferences from the original study: (1) 20 randomly drawn scenes per participant instead of all 297 points; (2) online instead of a lab slide show, with at least 3 s per scene instead of a 10 s limit; (3) the four directions are shown together as a 2×2 grid with the car roof cropped; (4) a background question (planning, architecture, landscape or other) was added.",
